// Mickey Finn: the yellow-red-yellow bucktail streamer over a silver body.

id mickey-finn
name "Mickey Finn"
aliases "Red and Yellow Bucktail" "Assassin"
type streamer
imitates baitfish attractor
difficulty beginner
species trout salmon sea-trout
season january february march april may june july august september october november december
origin region="Eastern North America, by 1932"
description "A flat silver tinsel body with an oval silver rib, and a three-layer bucktail wing: yellow, a sparse red, and yellow again on top."
history """
    Its maker is unknown. It was first called the Red and Yellow Bucktail. Wikipedia, after
    Joseph Bates, traces it to eastern Canada in the late 19th century; Nick Karas thought a
    commercial tyer made it for William Mills of New York. In spring 1932 John Alden Knight was
    given one by Junior Vanderhoff on the Mianus River near Greenwich, Connecticut, and they
    called it the Assassin. Gregory Clark of the Toronto Star renamed it the Mickey Finn, and
    Knight's 1937 magazine article, which came out at the time of the New York Sportsmen's Show,
    made it hugely popular.
    """
fishing """
    A bright streamer with a long record for brook trout, and a favourite early in the season
    and in murky water. In Canada big ones on hooks up to 4/0 were trolled behind
    canoes, and it was one of the first flies Danish anglers used when they took up fly fishing
    for sea trout in the late 1960s.
    """
sources {
    "Wikipedia: Mickey Finn (fly)" url="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mickey_Finn_(fly)"
    "American Museum of Fly Fishing: A personal natural history of the Mickey Finn (Willard P. Greenwood II)" url="https://amff.org/personal-natural-history-mickey-finn/"
    "MidCurrent: How to tie a Mickey Finn (Tim Flagler)" url="https://midcurrent.com/?p=37998"
    "Ahrex Hooks: Streamers, bucktails and Mickey Finn, part II (Lars Chr. Bentsen)" url="https://ahrexhooks.com/2020/01/24/streamers-bucktails-and-mickey-finn-part-ii/"
}
// Photos for checking proportions while authoring (pnpm refcheck); never published.
references {
    "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/dd/MickeyFinnStreamer.jpg" page="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MickeyFinnStreamer.jpg" author="Mike Cline" license="Public domain" note="Side view in the vise"
    "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/97/Mickey_Finn_streamer.jpg" page="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mickey_Finn_streamer.jpg" author="DeliciousDunder" license="CC0" note="Side view, with a non-standard orange throat"
}

hook streamer-4xl size=6
thread black size="6/0"

materials {
    rib oval-tinsel color=silver
    body flat-tinsel color=silver
    lower bucktail color=yellow label="Yellow bucktail, a small bunch"
    middle bucktail color=red label="Red bucktail, sparse"
    upper bucktail color=yellow label="Yellow bucktail, a larger bunch"
}

steps {
    attach-thread at=0.9 note="One eye length behind the eye."
    wrap-thread to=0.0 note="Binding the rib along the underside for a level underbody."
    tie-in rib
    tie-in body
    wrap-thread to=0.9
    wind body to=0.9 turns=22 note="Touching turns, back to the bend and forward again."
    tie-off body
    wind rib to=0.9 turns=5 note="Evenly spaced, at about 45° to the shank."
    tie-off rib
    // Assumption: Flagler has the tips at the back of the bend; photos show them longer, so somewhat past it.
    tie-in lower length=4.3 angle=3 spread=12 note="Cleaned and stacked, on top of the shank."
    trim lower note="Butts cut at a shallow angle, so the head tapers."
    tie-in middle length=4.3 angle=6 spread=10 note="Tips level with the yellow."
    trim middle
    tie-in upper length=4.3 angle=9 spread=15
    trim upper
    whip-finish note="A small, tapered head, varnished."
}
